    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b45f3386-62be-58d5-b455-cf3c99fd56ac",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(5+x^2){y}^{′}+2y={e}^{x},\\,y(2)=-3$\n- **II.** $(2+x^2){y}^{′}+1y={e}^{x},\\,y(4)=-3$\n- **III.** $(5+x^2){y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(2)={y}^{′}(2)=-5$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(5+x^2){y}^{′}+2y={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=-3.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(2+x^2){y}^{′}+1y={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-3.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $(5+x^2){y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)={y}^{′}(2)=-5.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
